 "note": {
  "status": "draft",
  "text": "A power is a short way to write a number multiplied by itself: 3⁴ = 3 × 3 × 3 × 3 = 81. In 3⁴, 3 is the base and 4 is the index (or exponent). Say “3 to the power 4”.\nSpecial powers: a² is “a squared”, a³ is “a cubed”, a¹ = a.\nPowers of 10: 10² = 100, 10³ = 1000, 10⁶ = 1 000 000: the index counts the zeros.\nA negative base needs brackets: (−2)³ = (−2) × (−2) × (−2) = −8 and (−2)⁴ = 16. With a negative base, an even index gives a positive answer and an odd index gives a negative answer.",
  "checks": []
 },
 "worked_example": {
  "status": "draft",
  "vars": {
   "b": 2,
   "n": 5,
   "c": -3,
   "m": 3
  },
  "problem": "Work out {pw(b, n)} and {pw(c, m)}.",
  "steps": [
   "{pw(b, n)} = {powExpand(b, n)} = {b ^ n}.",
   "{pw(c, m)} = {powExpand(c, m)}.",
   "The base is negative and the index {m} is odd, so the answer is negative: {pw(c, m)} = {c ^ m}."
  ],
  "answer": "{pw(b, n)} = {b ^ n} and {pw(c, m)} = {c ^ m}.",
  "checks": [
   "b ^ n == 32",
   "c ^ m == -27"
  ]
 },
